course: dictionary
lesson: 3247
title: Words for fast spreading
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: An epidemic is a word for something that spreads quickly to many people at the same time. The word around it gives you clues. Read this: «The Black Death of the 14th century probably started in Central Asia and spread along trade routes to Europe; China also suffered epidemics in that century.» Words like spread, routes and suffered tell you that epidemics are harmful and travel from place to place.
explain: Epidemic can also mean a sudden, fast spread of something bad, like bullying or a craze. Read «an epidemic of» with care: the words after it name the problem. You could say «an epidemic of bullying» or «an epidemic of new games at break», and the meaning stays the same: a lot of it, spreading fast.
explain: Try the swap check. Put your meaning in the sentence in place of the hard word. If «a flu epidemic» becomes «a flu that spreads quickly to many people», the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: An epidemic is a disease that spreads quickly to many people in one area at the same time.
fact: «An epidemic of» can also mean a sudden, fast spread of something bad, like bullying or a craze.
fact: As an adjective, epidemic means happening widely and fast.
fact: «The Black Death» spread along trade routes to Europe, and China also suffered epidemics in that century.
fact: Phrases to know: «a flu epidemic» and «epidemic proportions», which means very large and widespread.
exercise: «China also suffered epidemics in that century.» What does epidemics mean here? | * Diseases spreading quickly to many people at once | Long holidays by the sea | Trade routes across the desert | Sunny days with no rain
exercise: In the news: «an epidemic of» bullying hit one school. What does it mean? | A prize for the best school | A quiet afternoon with no problems | A new game played at break | * A fast spread of bullying that reaches many children
exercise: Find the word in this sentence that means a disease spreading to many people: «The Black Death... China also suffered epidemics in that century.» | Europe | Black Death | Central | * epidemics
test: Why do scientists study epidemics? | * To learn how germs spread and how to stop them | To make maps of the moon | To learn how to paint faster | To count how many children like sweets
test: In maths, what can epidemic numbers do? | Shrink to zero in one minute | Stay the same for ever | Turn into words | * Double quickly
exam: Which word could replace epidemic in «a sudden epidemic of new games at break»? | sleep | lesson | calm | * craze
